Very disturbing for Democrats is that Sayed is getting weaker even as Benson is gaining in support. The more that Michigan voters get to know Sayed, the less they like him.  Sayed was leading Rogers by 5.5% in the August edition of the MSU poll, a lead which Rogers has now more than cut in half.  Benson had a lead of 9% back in August, and has added almost 3.5 points to that margin.

This phenomenon may be attributed to Sayed’s stubborn and testy refusal to explain his relation with the Muslim Brotherhood, despite his 2012 endorsement of Mohammed Morsi, officially the Brotherhood’s winning candidate for president of Egypt after the Arab spring who soon tried to make himself into a dictator through an autogolpe.  At one of his recent town halls, Sayed angrily hectored and cross-examined a young man named Tom who had asked him the obvious questions about the Brotherhood.  Sayed seemed determined to prove that Tom was acting out of “Islamophobia,” the Brotherhood’s all-purpose defense against even the slightest criticism of their activities and ideology.  Something similar happened to Fox News correspondent Brett Baer when he dared to broach the topic of the Brothers. (But on National Public Radio’s weekly 1A International Hour, moderator Todd Zwillich and panelist Wendy Benjaminson of Bloomberg news agreed that Sayed is indeed the target of “rank bigotry” and [inevitably] "islamophobia." Qatar influencer Yusuf Qaradawi and the Ramadan clan, who developed this all-purpose rejoinder, would be proud of how effective such a word can be.)

Sayed has been showing a political ad featuring brief film clips of all the Fox News Channel stars who have expressed admiration for him, including the infamous extremist Tucker Carlson, who has endorsed him.  Also this week, Cenk Uygur’s Rebellion PAC begin showing a political ad which depicts a husband and wife lamenting that they cannot buy more food items at the supermarket because so much money has to be sent to Israel for the purpose of killing Palestinians. Sayed’s campaign responded to inquiries about this ad simply by saying that they had nothing to do with it, but stopping well short of condemning the contents. Uygur happens to be the uncle of the infamous Hassan Piker, the rabble rousing influencer who campaigned with Sayed during the Democratic primary, and who has branded Israel as a far greater threat to the United States than Hamas, while also opining that the United States deserved the tragic events of September 11, 2001. It should be obvious that the rhetorical ploy of blaming Jews for bad economic conditions or other misfortunes was a staple of totalitarian propaganda in central Europe during the fascist era, especially in Germany, and cannot be tolerated in modern America. This was an ad that Sayed needed to categorically repudiate, but he has so far failed to do so.

