AI BUBBLE NOW WIDELY SEEN AS ENDANGERED DANGER

During the past week, a number of market developments have suggested some growing dangers for the four year-long AI stock bubble.  A group of hackers, presumably from North Korea, were able to penetrate the security systems of Binance, said to be the world’s largest crypto exchange in terms of daily volume, and steal as much as $6 billion from crypto wallets, although later reports suggested that as much as $2 billion might have been frozen in place before the thieves could make off with it.

The presence of North Korean hackers is first of all a sign of growing aggressively on the part of the totalitarian CRINKs bloc.  We recall that, in his memoir of the 2008 subprime derivatives crisis, US Treasury Secretary Henry Paulsen reported that the Russian government had approached the Beijing regime with a proposal to create generalized financial panic in the United States through a speculative attack on agency bonds of the federal government.  At that time, the Chinese had declined the proposal, citing their extensive holdings of US Treasury securities.  With the pathetic weakness of the Trump regime, and increasing instability in both China and Russia, this time the Chinese answer might be different.  Trump deludes himself that Putin, Xi, and Kim are his friends, but this does not correspond to reality.

The Binance theft is considered to have been the largest such operation in all of human history. Certainly many of the exaggerated claims of crypto safety have been refuted.  Also this week, the tech company Alphabet, one of the magnificent seven and the parent company of Google, has reported a decline in earnings because of a situation in which less profitable Google AI searches have been crowding out the company’s main profit source in online search engine service.  The very idea that the invincible magic of AI could lead to losses after the recent colossal capex expenditures (450 billion by Google alone) was a shock to Wall Street, sending Alphabet shares to a loss of about 12%, reportedly the biggest one-day decline in the history of the company. Markets were roiled.

These events have triggered what appears to be an attempted broad-based risk-off move by many investors who are seeking to diversify away from AI stocks. The previously subversive and heretical idea that the four-year AI boom may not pay off in terms of increased profitability had long been banned from polite conversations on Wall Street, but now appears as an entirely plausible opinion. This may of course signal that the prolonged AI stock mania may now be coming to an end, and that this critical suspension of disbelief, which had long dominated financial discourse, may soon vanish.

In the October 30 edition of The Tech ReportTR, leading AI skeptic Ed Zitron cited reports that the investment management firm Blackstone is telling investors that Anthropic, one of the two leading AI companies, may not secure an investment grade credit rating for its bonded debt after its initial public offering (IPO) of stock shares, expected before the end of the year.  Corporate and other bonds are considered to be of investment grade if they receive ratings at or above BBB- from the ratings agencies Standard & Poor’s or Fitch, or Baa3 from Moody’s.  If corporate bonds are not of investment grade, they are then considered junk bonds, meaning high risk securities that carry a higher interest rate to compensate any buyer for the risk that the company behind them might default. If Anthropic, considered the most highly rated blue-chip corporation in the AI universe, is to be relegated to junk-bond status, then some rough days indeed are ahead for the Anthropic IPO and for the AI bubble in general. Zitron also cited Anthropic’s recently submitted S-1 report as containing little cause for optimism. (According to Investopedia, SEC Form S-1 is a registration document that U.S. companies file with the SEC to publicly disclose financial information before an initial public offering aka IPO.)

The implications are also grim for Sam Altman’s OpenAI, which is considered financially weaker than its competitor Anthropic. Altman has wisely postponed the OpenAI IPO until sometime next year, but there is little reason to believe that the company’s financial condition will materially improve over the coming months.  Investors might already be preparing for a wild ride on Wall Street, including by assuming a more defensive posture -- especially given Trump’s constant refrain that he intends to escalate the Iran war after the November election.

