Whether Vance knows it or not, rejection of our First Amendment separation of church (or mosque or synagogue or temple) and state amounts to a frontal attack on Western civilization, since it is this doctrine which has historically separated Western Civilization from its rival civilizations, including the Byzantine Empire, Putin’s “Russian world,” the Ottoman Turkish Caliphate, the legalist oriental despotism of China, or the Islamist regime in Iran today, since in all these religion and the state, religion and politics, are totally merged. That system of having the emperor and the pope in the same person is called caesaropapism, and it has failed whenever it was tried. The Russian Orthodox Patriarch Kirill or Cyril is a stooge appointed by Putin and reputedly a KGB agent. In Tehran, the top Ayatollah has been the supreme leader and dictator. In those unhappy places, nobody else needs to think about or decide anything, since it has all been decided for them down to the smallest detail. They only need to obey. Caesaropapism wipes out human freedom.

Here in the West, Pope Leo XIV is one person, while Trump or any other head of state or government is an entirely separate figure, and they often fight. That is the genius of Western Civilization, since when pope and president or pope and king disagree about important matters, even the humblest person can be forced think about that disagreement and decide where they stand. Where pope and dictator disagree, totalitarianism (government control of all aspects of life, down to the most minute detail, as practiced by Mussolini, Hitler, Stalin, and Mao) becomes far more difficult to impose.

Separation of church and state started right after the fall of the Roman Empire in the West in 476 AD. It was asserted by the African-born Pope Saint Gelasius I (reigned 492-496 AD) in his landmark 494 apostolic letter Duo Sunt (There Are Two), in which he propounded the idea that the spiritual and religious power of the Roman Pope is separate from (and superior to) the political, military, and temporal power of any emperor or king, and that these two powers are necessary but cannot be exercised by the same person. Here is the Western concept of the separation of church and state. This letter was sent to the Byzantine Emperor Anastasius and his servant, the Patriarch of Constantinople. The Byzantine Emperor wanted to concentrate all power, east and west, political and temporal, Rome and Byzantium, in his own hands. So it can be argued that Gelasius was a founding father of Western Civilization, building on related efforts like the first theory of the human individual previously set forth by another African founding father, Aurelius Augustinus aka St. Augustine.

Centuries later, the argument for the separation of church and state was re-asserted by the immortal statesman and poet Dante Alighieri (1265-1321). In his Divine Comedy, Dante asserted that a healthy civilization required two suns, a political and temporal power to illuminate the right path of human affairs on earth, and a spiritual and religious power to illuminate the path to God. He explained that the crisis of his time reflected the brutal takeover of the Roman papacy by the Kings of France like Philip the Fair, who in 1303 had his generals arrest and imprison Pope Boniface VIII, who soon died from wounds. With this, the sword of temporal power had broken the bishop’s crozier of religious power, and prolonged crisis was the result.

Does Vance want the United States to become a theocracy, where priests and bishops run the government? That was tried in the states of the church, and it failed amidst scandalous corruption. Does he want the US to imitate Moscow? There Patriarch Cyril is as much a part of Putin’s war machine as a missile, a tank, or a drone, and the Ukrainians wisely have broken away. Does he want the Tehran model? There Ayatollahs Khomeini and Kamenei presided over massacres of political opponents and unleashed terrorism on the world.

Whether Vance and Trump like it or not, the United States is not and never will be a land where churches and the state merge into a caesaropapist or theocratic regime. When before 1077 the German Emperor Henry IV tried to claim the power of appointing bishops, thus subjecting the church to his own authority, Pope Gregory VII (also known as Hildebrand) excommunicated Henry IV, putting his crown in acute danger from rivals. Henry decided he had no choice but to beg the pope to absolve him of his sin and lift the excommunication. He traveled across the Alps in winter to the pope’s castle at Canossa in northern Italy, where he was forced to kneel as a penitent in the snow for three days and three nights from January 25 to 28 1077 in one of the most dramatic events of the European Middle Ages, before Hildebrand relented and ended the excommunication.

Who knows if Vance’s remark about ending the separation of church and state will be seen by Pope Leo as a heresy and worthy of excommunication. If he does, and if Vance wants to continue as a Catholic, maybe the Veep will have to go to Canossa and beg for absolution.
